{"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLArrow\/Style\/single","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"metadata":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"case"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"single","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","externalID":"s:7IvorMXL8MXLArrowV5StyleO6singleyA2EmF","symbolKind":"case","modules":[{"name":"IvorMXL"}],"title":"MXLArrow.Style.single","roleHeading":"Case"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLArrow","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLArrow\/Style"]]},"sections":[],"kind":"symbol","schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"major":0,"minor":3},"abstract":[{"text":"A single arrow.","type":"text"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"platforms":["macOS"],"tokens":[{"text":"case","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"single","kind":"identifier"}],"languages":["swift"]}]}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlarrow\/style\/single"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"references":{"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLArrow/Style/single":{"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlarrow\/style\/single","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLArrow\/Style\/single","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"case"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"single"}],"title":"MXLArrow.Style.single","abstract":[{"text":"A single arrow.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","role":"symbol"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLArrow":{"abstract":[{"text":"The arrow element represents an arrow used for a musical technical indication. It can represent both Unicode","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"and SMuFL arrows. The presence of an arrowhead element indicates that only the arrowhead is displayed, not the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"arrow stem. The smufl attribute distinguishes different SMuFL glyphs that have an arrow appearance such as","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"arrowBlackUp, guitarStrumUp, or handbellsSwingUp. The specified glyph should match the descriptive","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"representation.","type":"text"}],"title":"MXLArrow","kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLArrow"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLArrow","kind":"identifier"}],"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlarrow","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLArrow"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLArrow/Style":{"title":"MXLArrow.Style","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"enum","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Style","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlarrow\/style","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLArrow\/Style","abstract":[{"text":"The arrow-style type represents the style of an arrow, using Unicode arrow terminology. Filled and hollow","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"arrows indicate polygonal single arrows. Paired arrows are duplicate single arrows in the same direction.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Combined arrows apply to double direction arrows like left right, indicating that an arrow in one direction","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"should be combined with an arrow in the other direction.","type":"text"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Style","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A MusicXML parser, normalizer, validator, and formatter."}],"title":"IvorMXL","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl","type":"topic","role":"collection"}}}